.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt KLDLOAD-ESTATE 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldload-estate
.Nd compare what every subsystem believes about each machine, and report where they disagree
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -table
.Op Fl -drift
.Op Fl -probe
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Every layer of a kldload host tells the truth about itself: libvirt knows
which machines run, the state database which were made and what they are,
DHCP which addresses were handed out, WireGuard which peers handshake,
Kubernetes which nodes are Ready.
Nothing else compares them, and a machine can be wrong only in the gap:
Ready in Kubernetes and absent from the mesh, running and invisible to
Ansible.
.Nm
asks each, puts the answers side by side per machine, and reports the
disagreements \(em the drift.
.Pp
The sources, each asked with a time limit, and skipped when absent:
.Bl -tag -width "state database"
.It libvirt
The system instance: every domain and its power state, and the DHCP
leases of its networks.
.It state database
Through
.Xr kldload-db 8 :
each machine's class, cluster, recorded address and mesh identity.
.It addresses
.Xr kldload-vm-ip 8 ,
the resolver the kldload tools share.
.It WireGuard
Every peer on every interface, by key, mesh address and endpoint.
.It mesh members
The members files the per-appliance and lab meshes write, so a machine
that is off still counts as on its mesh.
.It Kubernetes
Node readiness and the address each kubelet advertises, when the
kubeconfig's apiserver answers within 1.5 seconds.
.El
.Pp
A machine's address is, in order, what its kubelet advertises, what the
resolver finds, what the database recorded, and its lease.
It is
.Em targetable
when it runs and has an address.
.Pp
The findings:
.Bl -tag -width "stale-address"
.It Li mesh-missing
A Kubernetes node that runs with an address no WireGuard peer covers.
.It Li no-address
A running machine with no known address: Ansible cannot reach it.
.It Li stale-address
A machine that is not running but still has an address recorded, which
DHCP may have given to another.
.It Li unregistered
A domain libvirt knows and neither the database nor Kubernetes does.
.El
Each comes with the command that repairs it.
.Pp
Run it as root: a user cannot read WireGuard, and the table says so.
.Xr kld 1
and the web console run it as root.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -table
A table and the findings, for reading.
Without it the output is JSON:
.Li machines ,
.Li drift
and
.Li sources ,
which says which sources answered.
.It Fl -drift
Only the findings; exit 1 when there is one.
.It Fl -probe
Also log in to each running machine with an address over
.Xr ssh 1 ,
as root, which is what Ansible's success depends on; slow on a large
estate.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the usage and exit 0.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
Reported; with
.Fl -drift ,
no finding.
.It 1
With
.Fl -drift :
at least one finding.
.It 2
A usage error.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Printed on a kldload desktop with 51 machines known to one source or
another, none running, and no cluster or mesh up, on 2026-09-29.
.Ss The table (--table)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-estate --table
  MACHINE                 CLASS           POWER      ADDRESS          MESH   K8S       TARGET
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  app-adguard-ho          vm              shut off   -                --     -         no
  app-icecast-st          vm              shut off   -                --     -         no
\&...
  klab-golden-rocky       klab-golden     shut off   -                --     -         no
\&...
  sshfix-debian-probe     vm              absent     -                --     -         no
  sshfix-k8s-probe        clone           absent     -                --     -         no
  vdi                     clone           shut off   -                --     -         no
  vdi-1                   vm              shut off   -                --     -         no
  web-golden              vm              shut off   -                --     -         no

  51 machine(s), 0 targetable by Ansible.

  DRIFT — 2 finding(s):

    [unregistered] debian-desktop-ud30
      libvirt knows this domain; the state DB does not
      fix: kldload-db vm-register --name debian-desktop-ud30 --role <class>

    [unregistered] klab-green-fedora
      libvirt knows this domain; the state DB does not
      fix: kldload-db vm-register --name klab-green-fedora --role <class>

  (no answer from: wireguard, kubernetes -- absent here, or unreadable)
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Pp
.Ql absent
is a machine the database lists and libvirt does not have.
.Ss Only the findings (--drift)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-estate --drift --table
[unregistered] debian-desktop-ud30: libvirt knows this domain; the state DB does not
  fix: kldload-db vm-register --name debian-desktop-ud30 --role <class>
[unregistered] klab-green-fedora: libvirt knows this domain; the state DB does not
  fix: kldload-db vm-register --name klab-green-fedora --role <class>
# (exited 1)
# kldload-estate --drift
[
  {
    "machine": "debian-desktop-ud30",
    "kind": "unregistered",
    "detail": "libvirt knows this domain; the state DB does not",
    "repair": "kldload-db vm-register --name debian-desktop-ud30 --role <class>"
  },
\&...
]
# (exited 1)
.Ed
.Ss JSON (the default)
One machine of the
.Li machines
list, and
.Li sources :
